th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-OR-Modelling/minimum-weight-perfect-matching.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-OR-Modelling/minimum-weight-perfect-matching.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_minimum_weight_perfect_matching_c07cc8f4_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The odd-degree vertices of a network are to be paired; the cost $d_{ij}$ of pairing $i$ and $j$ is their shortest-path distance. Why can the cheapest pairing not be found by repeatedly taking the cheapest remaining pair?","options":["The cheapest pair is always in the optimum, so repeating the step is optimal.","A locally cheap pair may leave expensive pairs for the remaining vertices.","The order of choosing pairs does not matter, as all pairings cost the same.","Repeating the step works for four vertices but fails for six or more."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fed1d4fdc49aff459ed88ede","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_minimum_weight_perfect_matching_c07cc8f4_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A network has exactly four odd-degree vertices $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, which are to be split into two pairs. How many different complete pairings are there to compare?","options":["6","3","4","24"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"110cbef52d1165f13738c573","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_minimum_weight_perfect_matching_c07cc8f4_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The odd-degree vertices of a network are to be paired; the cost $d_{ij}$ of pairing $i$ and $j$ is their shortest-path distance. The model has binary variables $x_{ij}$ only for $i<j$. What is the reason?","options":["Pairs may only be linked from the smaller to the larger index.","Pairs with $i>j$ are forbidden, which makes some pairs impossible.","Shortest paths from $i$ to $j$ may differ from those from $j$ to $i$.","A pair is unordered; one variable per pair avoids counting it twice."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e24307685125dd8a93c5ad24","time_limit":45,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_minimum_weight_perfect_matching_c07cc8f4_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When the odd-degree vertices of a network are paired and each pair is to be linked by a route in the network, why is the pair cost $d_{ij}$ taken to be the shortest-path distance between $i$ and $j$?","options":["Pairs must be joined by a direct edge, so $d_{ij}$ is the edge length.","Any route may link the pair, and the cheapest route has that length.","The cost should count edges, so the route with fewest edges is used.","The average length of all routes is used to treat every route fairly."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9ea5a727fd32ddc25d24d6af","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}